277:
Feral cat colony caregivers shall be entitled to maintain and care for feral cats by providing food, water, shelter and other forms of sustenance, provided that the feral cat colonies are registered with a registered feral cat caregiver and that the feral cat colony caregiver takes all appropriate and available steps to meet the terms and conditions of this article.
Caregiver requirements. It shall be the duty of the caregivers to:
• Help to resolve any complaints over the conduct of a feral cat colony caregiver or of cats within a colony;
• Maintain records provided on the size and location of the colonies as well as the vaccination and spay and neuter records of cats in the caregivers colonies;
• Report annually to the department on the following:
• Number and location by street name or venue of colonies for which it acts as a sponsor in the city;
• Total number of cats in each of its colonies; and
• Number of cats from its colonies vaccinated, spayed and neutered pursuant to a TNR program, and number of cats and kittens from its colonies placed in permanent homes.
• Use due consideration to prevent feral cat colonies from being maintained on lands managed for wildlife or other natural resources, such as, but not limited to, nature preserves, where the presence of a feral cat colony is a proven threat, and to avoid the taking of rare, threatened or endangered species, as identified under the Georgia Endangered Species Protection Act;
• Provide any forms or other documentation necessary to allow feral cat colony to receive any public or private subsidies, medical care or other forms of assistance for their feral cat colonies which may be available to them.
Feral cat colony caregiver responsibilities. In order to be an approved managed feral cat colony caregiver, said caregivers shall be responsible for the following:
• Registering the colony;
• Taking all appropriate and available steps to spay or neuter colony cats and vaccinate for rabies;
• Ear-tipping the left or right ear of a colony cat that has been spayed or neutered and received a rabies vaccine so that colony cats can be readily identified;
• Providing food, water and, if feasible, shelter for colony cats;
• Obtaining proper veterinary attention for any colony cat that appears to require it;
• Observing the colony cats at least twice per week and keeping a record of any illness or unusual behavior noticed in any colony cat;
• Obtaining approval of the owner of any property, or any authorized representative of the owner, to which the caregiver requires access to provide colony care;
• Taking all responsible steps to: (1) remove kittens from the colony after they have been weaned; (2) place the kittens in home or foster homes for the purpose of subsequent permanent placement; and (3) capture and spay the mother cat;
• Reporting semi-annually in writing to the city council on: (1) the location of the colony; (2) the number and gender of all cats in the colony; (3) the number of cats that died or otherwise ceased being a part of the colony; (4) the number of kittens born to colony cats and their disposition; (5) the number of cats placed in animal shelters or in permanent homes as companion cats; and (6) the number of cats spayed or neutered; and
Withdrawal of feral cat colony caregiver or sponsor. In the event that a feral cat colony caregiver is unable or unwilling to continue in that role, he or she shall notify city council. In the event a caregiver is unable or unwilling to continue to perform its role, it shall so advise the department. The council shall work with the public to obtain a replacement caregiver.
Disposition of feral cat colony cats. An animal control officer who has trapped a cat whose ear has been tipped or which bears some other distinguishing mark, such as, but not limited to, a tattoo, indicating it belongs to a feral cat colony, shall take reasonable steps to notify the caregiver of the description and sex of the cat and, if available, the address or location where the cat was trapped. The caregiver shall then take all appropriate and available steps to return this cat to its colony.
If the feral cat colony caregiver is not able to immediately take custody of the cat or if the animal control officer cannot identify the colony caregiver immediately, the officer shall transport the cat to the animal control facility. The feral cat colony caregiver shall be responsible for retrieving the cat from the shelter within three (3) business days or advising the shelter if he or she does not intend to retrieve the cat; and
The department, its designee or a licensed veterinarian, in accordance with the following section, shall be the only persons permitted to destroy a feral cat. No person may cause the destruction by any means of a feral cat. The only exception will be by written permit from the Georgia Department of Agriculture for the purpose of controlling diseases transmissible to humans or other animals and only when all other methods and means have been exhausted. Any drugs used for the euthanasia shall be by or under the direction of a licensed veterinarian.
(Ord. No. 257, 9-14-2021)

307:
