384:
Water furnished by the city of Sparta shall be used for consumption by the consumer, members of his household and employees only. The consumer shall not sell water to any person or permit any other person to use said water. Water shall not be used for irrigation, fire protection, nor other purposes, except when water is available in quantity without interfering with the regular domestic consumption in the area served. Disregard for this rule shall be sufficient cause for refusal and/or discontinuance of service.
A. Where meter or meter box is placed on the premises of consumer, a suitable place shall be provided by the consumer therefor, unobstructed and accessible at all times to the meter reader.
B. The consumer shall furnish and maintain a private cut-off valve on the consumer's side of the meter.
C. The consumer's piping and apparatus shall be installed and maintained by the consumer at the consumer's expense, in a safe and efficient manner, and in accordance with the sanitary regulations of the State Health Department.
D. In order to be received as a consumer and entitled to receive water from the city's water system, all applicants must offer proof that any private wells located on their property are not physically connected to the lines of the city's water system and all applicants by becoming consumers of the city's covenant and agree that so long as they continue to be consumers of the city they will not permit the connection of any private wells on their property to the city's water system. The consumer shall additionally not discharge any wastewater to the sewer system not generated by potable water furnished by the city of Sparta's water system.
E. The consumer agrees to limit its discharge into the sewerage system that strength of sewage defined by trade usage as "domestic sewage." The limits of acceptable discharge shall be as follows:
1. The following wastes will be strictly inadmissible:
a. Wastes which create a fire or explosion hazard in the sewage or sewerage plant;
b. Waste produces which will impair the hydraulic capacity of the sewer system; and
c. Wastes which, in any quantity, create a hazard to people, to the sewer system, to the treatment process or to the receiving waters.
Examples of the above are:
a. Gasoline, fuel line, cleaning solvents, and paint vehicles;
b. Ashes, sand, cinders, metal, broken glass, wood metal shavings, rage and unshredded garbage; and
c. Hydrogen sulfide, sulfur dioxide, cyanides, acids and caustics.
2. The maximum permissible temperature of any discharge shall be one hundred fifty (150) degrees F (sixty-five (65) degrees C).
3. Volatile oils shall not be allowed in any amount. Mineral oil shall be removed to the maximum degree practical before discharge. Vegetable oils, fats, and grease shall be limited to no more than fifteen (15) milligrams per liter (mg/l).
4. The ph of influent waste shall be between 5.5 and 9.5.
5. Substances toxic to biological wastewater treatment processes such as copper, chromium, lead, zinc, arsenic, and nickel may also be limited if said substances are present in sufficient quantity adversely to affect treatment. Acceptable amounts of said substances shall be as follows unless otherwise determined by independent study and accepted by the city:
a. Nickel equal or less than five (5) mg/l;
b. Zinc equal or less than ten (10) mg/l;
c. Chromium (+3) equal or less than ten (10) mg/l;
d. Chromium (+6) equal or less than 0.05 mg/l;
e. Chlorine residual equal or less than 0.5 mg/l;
f. Cyanides - none allowed;
g. Cyanates - none allowed;
h. Isocyanates - none allowed;
i. All others - none allowed without an independent justifying study and tests accepted by the city.
6. The maximum allowable five-day biochemical oxygen demand (BOD) that will be accepted without surcharge payments shall be two hundred fifty (250) milligrams per liter (mg/l).
7. The maximum allowable amount of suspended solids that will be accepted without surcharge payments shall be five hundred (500) mg/l.
(Ord. 210 § 7, 2000: Ord. 205 § 7)

388:
When water and sewer service is discontinued and all bills paid, the security deposit shall be refunded to the consumer by the city.
A. Upon discontinuance of service for nonpayment of bills, the security deposit will be applied by the city of Sparta toward the settlement of the account. Any balance will be refunded to the consumer; however, if the security deposit is insufficient to cover the bill, the city may proceed to collect the balance in the usual way provided by law for collection of debts.
B. Service disconnected for nonpayment of bills will be restored only after bills are paid in full, such security deposit as may be required by the mayor and council is made, and a service charge as quoted in Section 13.04.100(C) for each meter reconnected.
C. The city reserves a right to discontinue its service without notice for the following additional reasons:
1. To prevent fraud or abuse;
2. Consumer's willful disregard of the city's rules;
3. Emergency repairs;
4. Insufficiency of water supply due to circumstances beyond the city's control;
5. Legal processes;
6. Direction of public authorities;
7. Strike, riot, fire, flood, unavoidable accident.
(Ord. 210 § 11, 2000: Ord. 205 § 11)

